-## VII.4 Regions and their inference
-
-Ember has **no lifetime syntax**. Every reference and view carries a **region** — the set of program
-points at which it must be valid — and the compiler infers every region.
-
-* `[LT-5]` Regions of locals are inferred by non-lexical liveness (§XVIII.4). Diagnostics describe
-  them in source terms: "the borrow of `x` on line 12 is still needed on line 19".
-* `[LT-1]` *(changed in 0.9.9)* **Signature elision.** A **source parameter** (ODR-024) is:
-  * a parameter whose type is a reference or view, in any mode; or
-  * a borrowed or `mut` parameter whose type is not `Copy` even when each of its type parameters is
-    taken to be `Copy`: an `Array[T]`, `String`, `Map`, `Box`, `RefCell` or arena, a struct or enum
-    without `@derive(Copy)`, or a tuple or fixed array holding one of these.
-  A source parameter of the second kind is the caller's place (`[FN-1]`, `[BRW-8]`): a result that
-  borrows it may point anywhere in it, into its own storage or into storage it owns, and the call's
-  loan is on the argument (`[BCK-1]`), shared for a borrowed parameter and exclusive for `mut`. A
-  `Copy` parameter that is not a reference or view is not a source parameter (so neither is a plain
-  `x: T`, nor a callable parameter, `[CLO-3]`), and nor is an `owned` parameter that is not a
-  reference or view. Which parameters are sources depends only on the declared signature. In an
-  instance of a generic, a parameter whose type there is a reference or view is a source of that
-  instance too, of the first kind: `Tree[str].leaf(v: T)` may return a view of what `v` views, and
-  its callers, which call that instance, know it (ODR-048). Where rule 1 below ties the result to a
-  borrowed receiver, such a parameter is a source alongside it when its declared type names a type
-  parameter the declared result names too: the result's type says it can hold one, so
-  `Map[str, V].entry(k) -> MapEntry[K, V, H]` returns an entry holding `k`, while `get(q) ->
-  Option[ref V]` borrows only the map (ODR-069).
-  For a function whose result is a reference or view:
-  1. if it has a borrowed receiver (`self` or `mut self`), of any type, the result borrows from the
-     receiver;
-  2. else, if exactly one parameter is a source parameter, the result borrows from it;
-  3. else, the result borrows from **all** source parameters together (the caller keeps all of them
-     borrowed while the result lives).
-  Rule 3 always type-checks; it may borrow more than the function needs. A returned reference or view
-  that borrows any other parameter is rejected: `E3060` when that parameter is `owned` or is passed
-  as a copy (`[BRW-8]`), and `E3062` otherwise.
-* `[LT-1a]` *(changed in 0.9.9)* *(changed in 0.9.10)* `@borrows(p, …)`, on its own line before the function, replaces the rule the compiler would
-  apply: the result borrows from exactly the named parameters (`self` names the receiver). Returning
-  a value that borrows from anything else is `E3062`. Each named parameter is a source parameter (`[LT-1]`)
-  or a `mut` parameter; naming a borrowed `Copy` parameter, or an `owned` parameter that is not a
-  reference or view, is `E2031`. `self.f` names a field `f` of the receiver whose type is a reference
-  or view, the receiver passed in any mode (`self`, `mut self`, `owned self`, or as a copy,
-  `[BRW-8]`): the result borrows what `f` borrows, and the call's loan on the receiver ends with the
-  call. A result read through a `ref mut` field borrows `self` as well, so it cannot name the field
-  alone (`E3062`): an iterator gives no two mutable items. `f` may instead be a field whose type is a
-  type parameter, with a result whose type names one (`Option[I.Item]`); each instance is checked, as
-  a source is (`[LT-1]`, ODR-048): where the instance's field and result are views the result borrows
-  what the field borrows, and where they are not it borrows nothing. Naming `self.f` with no
-  receiver, or a field of neither kind, is `E2031`. The standard library's iterators that read
-  through a shared field declare it on `next`, and its iterator adapters on the iterator they wrap,
-  so `for k in m:` and `m.keys()` yield references that borrow the map (`[CTL-1]`), which a program
-  may keep across turns and after the loop. It is needed only where rule 1 or 3 borrows more than
-  the caller can afford, or to let the result borrow a `mut` parameter of a `Copy` type:
+* `[BRW-6]` *(changed in 0.9.15)* **Lending a `ref mut` name.** From a `ref mut` name `r`, a read-only
+  view of `r.f` keeps `r` from changing `r.f` while it is used, and `ref mut r.f` lends that part, so `r`
+  does not reach it meanwhile. Passing a `ref mut` name to a `ref mut` parameter (`bump(ref mut r)`)
+  lends it for the call; it does not hand it over.
+* `[BRW-7]` *(changed in 0.9.15)* Using a place that is uninitialised on some path, one never given a value there, is `E3050`;
+  using one a hand-over may have reached is `[OWN-3]`'s `E3040` (ODR-114).
+* `[BRW-8]` *(changed in 0.9.9)* *(changed in 0.9.15)* A plain or `ref mut` parameter is passed by
+  address: the callee reads, or changes, the caller's place (`[FN-1]`, ODR-024). Two kinds are passed as
+  a copy instead:
+  * a view (a `ref`, `Span`, `MutSpan`, `StrView` or `@view` struct), which is passed as itself and keeps
+    the regions it carries;
+  * a plain parameter of a `Copy` type that holds no `Cell` or `UnsafeCell`, which goes in registers when
+    it is no larger than two pointers, except the object of a method whose result is a view (`[LT-1]`
+    rule 1).
+  An `owns` parameter receives the value. A view into the own storage of a parameter passed as a copy
+  ends with the call: returning it is `E3063`. The choice depends only on the declared first line, never
+  on the body; it is an ABI decision and cannot be observed. An instance of a generic function, method
+  or type has the generic's declared first line: a parameter its result may point into (the object, or
+  a source parameter of `[LT-1]`'s second kind) is passed as the declaration passes it, whatever the
+  type arguments, so `Holder[StrView]`'s object is passed by address as `Holder[T]`'s is (ODR-048).
+* `[BRW-9]` *(changed in 0.9.15)* A view in Safe code is never null, dangling or unaligned.
